rac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/3cb8903ed53a713c5e23e4dbad4801788440954ac3a5f7e08ab1bd71cc9c631f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326572 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326573 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326574 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326575 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326576 13194139535012 true] [17592186326571 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535012